 # The phase profile: which part of a query moved
 
-<!-- TODO: one paragraph, in this project's voice, on why a budget that only says "this got slower"
-is a number nobody can act on, and what a count per phase adds to it. Say that nothing on this page
-is a measurement of the machine — every number here is work the engine did — and link the counter the
-profile is read from (`../src/engine/trace/counters.rs`). -->
+A budget that says "this query got slower" is a number nobody can act on. `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`
+made one cost of this store a number — `pruning_rate`, the share of the segments a query did not have
+to open — and that answers *how much was saved*. What it does not answer is the question a red budget
+actually asks: **which part of the run is now doing the extra work?** A query whose prune decision
+stopped pruning, a segment format that got more expensive to inflate, a predicate that keeps ten times
+as many records as it used to and a `GROUP BY …, series` that walks them once per series all show up
+as the same thing in a wall-clock number, and as four different things in the profile this page is
+about.
 
-[TODO](TODO)
+So every run of a query counts what it did, phase by phase, and answers with those counts beside its
+answer. The rules live in [the trace module](../src/engine/trace/counters.rs); the counting happens
+where the work does, in [the executor](../src/engine/query/exec.rs); and the lane that prints a
+profile of a real store is [the course's harness](../tests/attribution.rs). If this page and those
+files disagree, the files are right and this page is a bug.
+
+Nothing on this page is a measurement of the machine. Every number is a count of something the engine
+did — a segment examined, a record decoded, a value folded into a window — so two runs of the same
+question over the same store answer with the same profile in any process, on any machine, under any
+load. That is the property `jobq/3.22-bench-budget` argues for and the reason this project budgets
+counts at all.
 
 ## The phases
 
-<!-- TODO: what each of the seven phases counts, and — the part a reader needs — the rule that
-decides when it is charged. A table with one row per phase is what the harness prints: `headers`,
-`blocks`, `decompress`, `decode`, `filter`, `aggregate`, `render`. Say which phase a query pays for
-whether or not it reads a segment, and which one is the predicate rather than the range. -->
+Seven, in the order a query walks them. The name in the first column is what a metric line and a
+JSON key spell it with.
+
+| phase | charged once per | what a bigger number means |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `headers` | segment the plan examined | the query was planned against a longer store, or the plan stopped pruning |
+| `blocks` | segment file whose block was read | the plan kept more segments: less was pruned, by the range or by the index |
+| `decompress` | block inflated | the same read: every block that was read is inflated, and one whose seal refuses it is read and never inflated |
+| `decode` | record read out of a block | more records were read — more segments, or bigger ones |
+| `filter` | record the predicates kept | the predicates are matching more of what is read |
+| `aggregate` | value folded into a window | more values reached the windows: the range or the predicate widened |
+| `render` | bucket written into an answer | the answer covers more windows or more series |
+
+Three of the rules behind that table are decisions rather than descriptions:
 
-| phase | charged once per |
-| --- | --- |
-| TODO | TODO |
+- **`headers` is the only phase a query pays for whether or not it reads anything.** A plan is made
+  from the manifest alone (`lakelet/4.11-query-engine`), and making it means examining every segment's
+  stated span and, for the ones the range meets, its sidecar index
+  (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`). A query whose range meets no segment costs exactly that many
+  headers and nothing else; a query whose range meets everything costs the same headers as one that
+  prunes it all away, because deciding is what the phase counts.
+- **`filter` is the predicate and not the range.** "The records `WHERE` kept" is this pass's
+  question; whether a kept record is inside the range is the window pass's, and a range that is not a
+  whole number of windows long has a first and a last window that reach past what the query asked for.
+  A record the predicate kept and the range did not is a **filtered** record that was never
+  aggregated, which is the difference between "the predicate got wider" and "the range got wider".
+- **`aggregate` is what the window pass folded in, not what it was handed.** A `GROUP BY …, series`
+  query hands the pass the records it read once per series — a scan per series, which is the design
+  `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` chose over a second implementation of the grid — so counting what was
+  offered would count the same record several times and stop meaning anything. The pass's own count of
+  what became part of a window is the honest one, and it is what the phase is charged with.
+
+A count of zero is a metric, not a missing line: "this run decoded nothing" is a fact about the run,
+and a profile that left the number out would leave a reader unable to tell a phase that did no work
+from a phase that was never asked.
 
 ## The identities
 
-<!-- TODO: the inequalities that make the profile a measurement rather than seven tallies, and the
-conservation identity that ties `headers` to the pruning counters `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`
-added. Say what each one would catch if it were broken. -->
+A counter no case can falsify is worse than no counter: it is a number a reader trusts and a defect
+does not move. The phases are therefore not seven independent tallies — each is a subset or a
+superset of another, and the relations are what make the profile checkable:
+
+```text
+headers >= blocks >= decompress                  every read block was decided about, and read before it was inflated
+decode  >= filter >= aggregate >= render         every decoded record is offered to the predicates,
+                                                 every kept one to the window pass, and a bucket holds at least one value
+headers == segments_opened + segments_pruned + blocks_pruned
+                                                 every segment the plan examined is opened or pruned —
+                                                 by the range or by the index — and never two of those
+decode  == rows_scanned                          the outcome's own count of what it read
+aggregate == rows_answered                       every value that was folded in landed in a bucket
+```
+
+The conservation line is the one that ties this page to the pruning counters: `blocks_pruned` is
+`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`'s share of the store, `segments_pruned` is `lakelet/4.11-query-engine`'s,
+and those two beside `segments_opened` are every segment the manifest names. A plan that reported a
+segment it never examined, or examined one it never reported, fails it — which is the failure a
+profile of seven plausible numbers would otherwise hide.
+
+What each identity catches, in the direction that matters:
+
+- `decode >= filter`: a filter charged for records that were pruned away is a predicate credited with
+  work the read never paid for;
+- `filter >= aggregate`: a window pass charged for values the predicate dropped is the "the range got
+  wider" story told about a predicate that did not change;
+- `aggregate >= render`: more buckets than values means a bucket was invented — the one defect in this
+  area that changes an answer rather than a number;
+- `headers >= blocks`: work read that the plan never decided about is pruning reported but not
+  performed.
 
 ## Running the lane
 
-<!-- TODO: the exact command, what it prints, and how to read one line of the course's baseline. Name
-the two queries the lane asks and what the difference between their profiles is worth. -->
+```sh
+% cargo test -p lakelet --test attribution -- --nocapture
+lakelet-budget selective_headers=24
+lakelet-budget selective_blocks=1
+lakelet-budget selective_decompress=1
+lakelet-budget selective_decode=60
+lakelet-budget selective_filter=30
+lakelet-budget selective_aggregate=30
+lakelet-budget selective_render=1
+lakelet-budget selective_share_decode=41
+…
+lakelet-budget wide_decode=720
+…
+lakelet-budget decode_saved=660
+lakelet-budget segments_saved=11
+lakelet-budget bytes_saved=2708
+lakelet-budget merged_total=1647
+lakelet-budget merged_runs=2
+lakelet-budget refused_max=0
+```
+
+The store is twenty-four sealed segments, one hour each and sixty records each: thirty samples of
+`cpu,host=h{i}`, a series exactly one segment carries, and thirty of `cpu,host=fleet`, a series every
+segment carries. The lane asks two questions of the last twelve hours, one window an hour:
+
+- **`selective`** asks for `h20`. The manifest rules twelve segments out by their stated spans and the
+  index rules eleven more out, so one segment is opened: 60 records decoded, 30 kept by the predicate,
+  30 folded into one bucket.
+- **`wide`** asks for `fleet`. Its range meets the same twelve segments and the index can rule none of
+  them out — a filter that answered "no" about a pair a segment really holds would be a lost record —
+  so twelve segments are opened: 720 records decoded, 360 kept, 360 folded into twelve buckets.
+
+The five `*_saved` lines are the difference between the two, and they are the lane's whole story: the
+index was worth eleven segment reads, 660 decoded records and 2708 bytes on this store, and it was
+worth exactly nothing in `render` — the wide query's answer has more buckets, and a profile that
+called that a saving would be reporting the wrong direction. `merged_total` and `merged_runs` are the
+two runs as one report (`Trace::merge`): the pair cost 1647 units of work over two runs, which is the
+selective profile plus the wide one and nothing else.
+
+The course keeps a baseline of every one of these lines and `kiln check
+lakelet/4.15-budget-regression --slow` compares the two: each metric within ±10%, and any name ending
+in `_max` judged as an upper bound instead. The baseline is content — the numbers the reference
+solution printed — and editing the harness or the baseline changes the instrument rather than the
+machine it measures.
 
 ## A red budget, read into a phase
 
-<!-- TODO: the walk a reader does when `slow:bench-budget@10` names a metric that moved: which
-profile to open first, which phase a bigger number belongs to, and what a phase that *stopped* being
-charged says about a run. Use numbers from this store. -->
+When the gate goes red it names the metrics that moved, and the profile is where they are read. The
+walk is short because the phases are ordered by how far into the run they happen:
+
+1. **`headers` moved and nothing else.** The plan is doing more deciding — the store grew, or a range
+   that used to be pruned now is not. No file was read, so the answer is about the manifest.
+2. **`blocks` or `decompress` moved with `decode`.** More segments were opened, and everything
+   downstream of the read moved with them. Look at the pruning: `segments_pruned` and `blocks_pruned`
+   in the same answer say whether the range or the index stopped doing its work.
+3. **`decode` moved and `blocks` did not.** The same number of files, more records in them. That is a
+   segment layout or a flush policy question, not a query one.
+4. **`filter` moved with `decode` and `aggregate` did not.** The predicates are keeping more of what
+   is read but the windows are unchanged: the query got wider, the range did not.
+5. **`filter` moved alone.** The same records were read and more of them passed the predicate. Look at
+   what `WHERE` selects — a tag that used to be rare, a series that has spread.
+6. **`aggregate` or `render` moved alone.** The answer's shape changed: more windows in the range, more
+   series in the group, or a bucket per series where there used to be a mixed answer.
+
+The drill that makes this concrete is one line long: take the index directory out of a store and ask
+the same two questions again. On the store above, measured with the harness:
+
+```text
+                 with the index        without it
+selective_decode            60              720
+selective_filter            30               30
+selective_aggregate         30               30
+selective_render             1                1
+wide_decode                 720             720
+wide_filter                 360             360
+```
+
+Twelve segments read where one was enough, and the same thirty records kept — the index saved *reads*
+and not records, which is exactly what the phase view says and what the answer alone could never say.
+The wide query is the same either way, and that is not a detail: with the index gone the wide profile
+does not move, so a reader who only looked at that run would conclude the index was worth nothing.
 
 ## Where the ideas come from
 
-<!-- TODO: name the two standing ideas this page stands on — a phase profile from a profiler, and a
-counter compared across two runs — and say what this project takes from each. -->
+Two ideas stand under this page, and both are older than this project:
+
+- **a phase profile.** `perf stat` reports a run as counts rather than a duration — instructions,
+  branches, cache misses — because a count is a property of the program and a duration is a property
+  of the machine. The seven phases here are the same idea at the scale of one query, with the phases
+  chosen by the parts of *this* store that a regression can hide in.
+- **a counter compared across two runs.** Prometheus's `process_cpu_seconds_total` and the counters
+  beside it are monotonically increasing totals that mean the same thing on every machine, so a
+  dashboard can subtract two samples and trust the difference. `decode_saved` is the same arithmetic
+  done once, in a gate, on purpose — against a baseline measured on a machine nobody had to name.
